  "assembly": "# Advent of Code 2025, day 11 part 1 (Reactor)\n#\n# THE PUZZLE\n# Each line of the input names a device and the devices its outputs lead to. Data flows only from a\n# device through its outputs. The answer is the number of different paths from the device \"you\" to the\n# device \"out\".\n#\n# THE IDEA\n# The number of paths from a device to \"out\" is the sum of that number for each of its outputs, and\n# \"out\" itself has 1. So the program works the numbers out from \"you\" downwards, and keeps the number\n# of every device it has finished: a device reached a second time, by another way, costs nothing more.\n# In the example: ddd, eee, fff, ggg and iii have 1 each; bbb = ddd + eee = 2; ccc = ddd + eee + fff = 3;\n# you = bbb + ccc = 5.\n# A device name is three letters. In memory it is one number: a = 0, b = 1, ..., z = 25, and the name\n# is (first * 26 + second) * 26 + third, so \"you\" is 16608 and \"out\" is 10003. A table with one word\n# for each of the 17,576 possible names says where the device's record is.\n#\n# THE WHOLE PROGRAM IN PSEUDO CODE\n#     for each line of the input: give the device a record; DEVICE[its name] = the record's address\n#     give \"out\" a record with PATHS = 1, marked done\n#     REC = DEVICE[\"you\"]; COUNT\n#     TOTAL = PATHS of \"you\"; write TOTAL as 15 decimal digits\n#     stop\n#\n#     COUNT:                                                # the record at REC gets its PATHS\n#         if REC is marked done: return\n#         for each output of the device:\n#             if there is a device of that name: keep REC and the place in the list; REC = its record; COUNT;\n#                 get them back; PATHS of REC = PATHS of REC + PATHS of the output\n#         mark REC done\n#\n# COUNT calls itself. What it must keep goes on the CPU's stack with psh and comes back with pop.\n#\n# NUMBERS OF FOUR WORDS\n# The number of paths can be far larger than a register holds. PATHS and TOTAL are four 16-bit words,\n# lowest first, added word by word: two words are added in a 32-bit register, the lower half of the sum\n# is the result's word and the upper half the carry into the next word.\n#\n# THE RESULT, when the CPU stops on the Breakpoint at END\n#   0x0100-0x0103  TOTAL as four 16-bit words, lowest first\n#   0x0110-0x011E  TOTAL as 15 decimal digits, one per word, most significant first\n#\n# THE INPUT (rule 1 of RULES.md), at 0x2000\n# Line after line: the device's name as a number, the names of its outputs, then 0xFFFF. After the\n# last line comes one more 0xFFFF.\n# Built-in limits: names of three small letters; at most 1,360 devices; the input ends before 0x6000;\n# no path comes back to a device it has left (the puzzle's \"data cannot flow backwards\");\n# the answer below 10^15.\n#\n# THE IDIOMS (v is a variable's address, below 0x1000)\n#     getA v / getB v          A or B = the word at v, upper half 0\n#     putA v                   the word at v = A   (this uses up B)\n#     getAat p / putAat p      the same for the word the pointer p points at\n#     getAoff p k              A = the word at the address (p + k); p is a variable holding an address\n#     copy v w                 w = v\n#     keepcarry v              v = the upper half of A: the carry of the sum just made\n#     if_eq L / if_neq L       compares A with B as they are now\n# What each instruction does exactly is in findings/cpu-instructions.md.\n#\n# MEMORY MAP\n#   0x0100-0x014F  variables            0x2000-  the input (576 lines end at 0x2AC6)\n#   0x1000-        the program          0x6000-  the records, six words each: the address of the device's\n#   0x1800-        the subroutines               outputs, 1 when done, PATHS (four words)\n#   0x1E00-        POWERS: 10^14 down to 1, four words each, then 0\n#   0x8000-0xC4A7  DEVICE: for each possible name, the address of the device's record, or 0\n#   0xE000-        the CPU's stack\n#   0xFF00         where the CPU starts\n\n# ---- The CPU's instructions as symbols (day 1's table without its _32 names, which the CPU ignores) ----\nsymbol nop 0b0000_0000_0000_0000            # the word 0: stops the CPU (the same word as non)\nsymbol ld  0b0010_0000_0000_0000            # load a register from memory (used through the load macro)\nsymbol lia 0b0011_0000_0000_0000            # lia n: A = n (0 to 4095), upper half 0\nsymbol lib 0b0100_0000_0000_0000            # lib n: B = n (0 to 4095), upper half 0\nsymbol sto 0b0101_0000_0000_0000            # store a register to memory (used through the store macro)\n\nmacro  load  { ld  | a? | (b? >> 6) }       # load X Y: X = the word at the address in Y\nmacro  store { sto | a? | (b? >> 6) }       # store X Y: the word at the address in Y = X\n\nsymbol sum    0b0110_0100_0000_0000         # A = A + B (32 bits), sets zero, carry, nega\nsymbol sub    0b0110_0010_0000_0000         # A = A - B (32 bits), sets zero, carry, nega\nsymbol shf    0b0110_0001_0000_0000         # shf | k: A = A shifted left by k (0 to 15)\nsymbol and    0b0110_0000_1000_0000         # A = A and B\nsymbol or     0b0110_0000_0100_0000         # A = A or B\nsymbol xor    0b0110_0000_0010_0000         # A = A xor B\nsymbol neg    0b0110_0000_0001_0000         # A = every bit of A flipped\n\nsymbol psh 0b1010_0000_0000_0000            # psh X: push the 16 bits of X\nsymbol pop 0b1011_0000_0000_0000            # pop X: pop 16 bits into X\n\nsymbol jmp  0b1100_0000_0000_0000           # jmp; L: go to L.  jmp c; L: go to L when c holds\nsymbol call 0b1110_0000_0000_0000           # call; L: run the subroutine at L\nsymbol ret  0b1111_0000_0000_0000           # return from a subroutine\n\n# The registers, as load, store, psh and pop name them\nsymbol rgAH 0b1000_0000_0000                # the upper 16 bits of A\nsymbol rgA  0b0100_0000_0000                # the lower 16 bits of A\nsymbol rgBH 0b0010_0000_0000                # the upper 16 bits of B\nsymbol rgB  0b0001_0000_0000                # the lower 16 bits of B\nsymbol rgSP 0b0000_1000_0000                # the program counter (leave alone)\nsymbol rgSB 0b0000_0100_0000                # the stack pointer (leave alone)\nsymbol non  0                               # stops the CPU (Breakpoint): the end of the program\n\n# The conditions of jmp\nsymbol zero  0b1000_0000_0000               # the last sum or sub gave 0\nsymbol carry 0b0100_0000_0000               # the last sum carried, or the last sub did not borrow (A >= B)\nsymbol nega  0b0010_0000_0000               # the last sub went below 0 (A < B)\nsymbol eq    0b0001_0000_0000               # A == B right now\nsymbol neq   0b0000_1000_0000               # A != B right now\n\n# ---- Macros for the common operations (explained in the README). A macro cannot use another macro, so\n#      load and store are spelled out here. v: a variable, p: a variable holding an address, L: a label ----\nmacro getA     { lia a?; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6) }                                              # A = v\nmacro getB     { lib a?; ld | rgB | (rgB >> 6) }                                              # B = v\nmacro putA     { lib a?; sto | rgA | (rgB >> 6) }                                             # v = A (uses B)\nmacro getAat   { lia a?; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6); ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6) }                       # A = the word p points at\nmacro putAat   { lib a?; ld | rgB | (rgB >> 6); sto | rgA | (rgB >> 6) }                      # the word p points at = A (uses B)\nmacro inc      { lia a?; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6); lib 1; sum; lib a?; sto | rgA | (rgB >> 6) }  # v = v + 1 (uses A and B)\nmacro goto     { jmp; a? }                                                                    # go to L\nmacro if_eq    { jmp eq; a? }                                                                 # go to L when A == B\nmacro do       { call; a? }                                                                   # call the subroutine L\nmacro if_carry { jmp carry; a? }                                                              # go to L after a sub when A >= B\nmacro getAoff  { lia a?; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6); lib b?; sum; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6) }                # A = the word at (p + k) (uses B)\nmacro copy     { lia a?; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6); lib b?; sto | rgA | (rgB >> 6) }                      # w = v (uses A and B)\nmacro get32A   { lib | (a? + 1); ld | rgAH | (rgB >> 6); lib a?; ld | rgA | (rgB >> 6) }                  # A = the 32 bits at v, v + 1 (uses B)\nmacro get32B   { lia | (a? + 1); ld | rgBH | (rgA >> 6); lia a?; ld | rgB | (rgA >> 6) }                  # B = the 32 bits at v, v + 1 (uses A)\nmacro get32AB  { lib | (a? + 1); ld | rgAH | (rgB >> 6); lib a?; ld | rgA | (rgB >> 6); psh rgAH; psh rgA; lia | (b? + 1); ld | rgBH | (rgA >> 6); lia b?; ld | rgB | (rgA >> 6); pop rgA; pop rgAH }\n# get32AB v w: A = the 32 bits at v, B = the 32 bits at w\nmacro put32A   { lib a?; sto | rgA | (rgB >> 6); lib | (a? + 1); sto | rgAH | (rgB >> 6) }                # the 32 bits at v, v + 1 = A (uses B)\n\nmacro keepcarry { psh rgAH; pop rgB; lia a?; sto | rgB | (rgA >> 6) }                                 # v = the upper half of A (uses A and B)\nmacro if_neq   { jmp neq; a? }                                                                # go to L when A != B\n\n\n# ---- Variables (all below 0x1000, so lia/lib can address them) ----\n# The result, four words: the number of paths\npointer TOTAL0 0x0100 0\npointer TOTAL1 0x0101 0\npointer TOTAL2 0x0102 0\npointer TOTAL3 0x0103 0\n# IN: the address of the next word of the input. NEXT_REC: the next free record\npointer IN 0x0120 0x2000\npointer NEXT_REC 0x0121 0x6000\n# REC: the record COUNT works on. LP: the place in its list of outputs. CHILD: the record of an output\npointer REC 0x0122 0\npointer LP 0x0123 0\npointer CHILD 0x0124 0\n# PA, PB: the words being added. ADD: the word from PB. ADR: an address worked out for a store\npointer PA 0x0125 0\npointer PB 0x0126 0\npointer ADD 0x0127 0\npointer ADR 0x0128 0\n# The carry from one word of an addition into the next\npointer CARRY 0x0129 0\n# SUBTRACT works out DIFF = X - Y, and CARRY = 1 when X >= Y (for the decimal digits)\npointer X0 0x012A 0\npointer X1 0x012B 0\npointer X2 0x012C 0\npointer X3 0x012D 0\npointer Y0 0x012E 0\npointer Y1 0x012F 0\npointer Y2 0x0130 0\npointer Y3 0x0131 0\npointer DIFF0 0x0132 0\npointer DIFF1 0x0133 0\npointer DIFF2 0x0134 0\npointer DIFF3 0x0135 0\n# For the decimal digits: how often the current power of ten fitted\npointer DIGIT_VALUE 0x0136 0\n# Pointers: DIGIT the next digit to write, POW the next power of ten\npointer DIGIT 0x0137 0x0110\npointer POW 0x0138 0x1E00\n# Constants that do not fit an instruction's place for a number. YOU and OUT are the names \"you\" and \"out\" as numbers\npointer DEVICE 0x0139 0x8000\npointer YOU 0x013A 16608\npointer OUT 0x013B 10003\npointer END_MARK 0x013C 0xFFFF\npointer ALL_ONES 0x013D 0xFFFF\n\n# ---- The CPU starts at 0xFF00 ----\norigin 0xFF00\ngoto MAIN\n\norigin 0x1000\n@ MAIN\n# ==== a record for every device ====\n#     while memory[IN] != 0xFFFF:                           # IN starts at the first line\n#         DEVICE[memory[IN]] = NEXT_REC\n#         IN = IN + 1; memory[NEXT_REC] = IN                # the record's first word: where the outputs are listed\n#         NEXT_REC = NEXT_REC + 6\n#         while memory[IN] != 0xFFFF: IN = IN + 1           # step over the outputs\n#         IN = IN + 1\n@ LINE\n\tgetAat IN; getB END_MARK                                     # A = the name of the device, B = 0xFFFF\n\tif_eq LINES_DONE                                             # the end of the input\n\tgetB DEVICE; sum                                             # A = the address of DEVICE[the name]\n\tgetB NEXT_REC; store rgB rgA                                 # DEVICE[the name] = NEXT_REC\n\tinc IN                                                       # IN = IN + 1\n\tputAat NEXT_REC                                              # memory[NEXT_REC] = IN (A still holds IN)\n\tgetA NEXT_REC; lib 6; sum; putA NEXT_REC                     # NEXT_REC = NEXT_REC + 6\n@ LINE_OUTPUT\n\tgetAat IN; getB END_MARK                                     # A = memory[IN], B = 0xFFFF\n\tif_eq LINE_END\n\tinc IN                                                       # IN = IN + 1\n\tgoto LINE_OUTPUT\n@ LINE_END\n\tinc IN                                                       # IN = IN + 1: past the 0xFFFF\n\tgoto LINE\n\n# ==== \"out\" has one path, and is done; then count from \"you\" ====\n#     DEVICE[OUT] = NEXT_REC; memory[NEXT_REC + 1] = 1; memory[NEXT_REC + 2] = 1\n#     REC = DEVICE[YOU]; COUNT\n#     TOTAL = PATHS of REC\n@ LINES_DONE\n\tgetA OUT; getB DEVICE; sum                                   # A = the address of DEVICE[\"out\"]\n\tgetB NEXT_REC; store rgB rgA                                 # DEVICE[\"out\"] = NEXT_REC\n\tgetA NEXT_REC; lib 1; sum; lib 1; store rgB rgA              # the record is done\n\tgetA NEXT_REC; lib 2; sum; lib 1; store rgB rgA              # its PATHS = 1 (the lowest word; the others are 0)\n\tgetA YOU; getB DEVICE; sum; load rgA rgA; putA REC           # REC = DEVICE[\"you\"]\n\tdo COUNT                                                     # PATHS of \"you\"\n\tgetAoff REC 2; putA TOTAL0                                   # TOTAL = PATHS of REC; word 0 (the lowest)\n\tgetAoff REC 3; putA TOTAL1                                   # word 1\n\tgetAoff REC 4; putA TOTAL2                                   # word 2\n\tgetAoff REC 5; putA TOTAL3                                   # word 3 (the top)\n\n# ==== write TOTAL as 15 decimal digits ====\n# A digit is how often a power of ten fits into what is left. Without division that is counting.\n# X is what is left of the total, Y the power of ten:\n#     X = TOTAL\n#     for Y in 10^14, 10^13, ..., 10, 1:                # the table ends at a number whose lowest word is 0\n#         DIGIT_VALUE = 0\n#         loop:\n#             SUBTRACT                                  # DIFF = X - Y, CARRY = 1 when X >= Y\n#             if CARRY == 0: break\n#             X = DIFF; DIGIT_VALUE = DIGIT_VALUE + 1\n#         memory[DIGIT] = DIGIT_VALUE; DIGIT = DIGIT + 1\n# (The lowest word of a power of ten up to 10^15 is never 0, so a 0 there can end the table.)\n@ WRITE_TOTAL\n\tcopy TOTAL0 X0                                               # X = TOTAL; word 0 (the lowest)\n\tcopy TOTAL1 X1                                               # word 1\n\tcopy TOTAL2 X2                                               # word 2\n\tcopy TOTAL3 X3                                               # word 3 (the top)\n@ NEXT_POWER\n\tgetAat POW                                                   # A = the lowest word of the next power of ten\n\tlib 0                                                        # B = 0\n\tif_eq END                                                    # a lowest word of 0 ends the table\n\tputA Y0                                                      # Y = the power of ten; word 0 (the lowest)\n\tgetAoff POW 1; putA Y1                                       # word 1\n\tgetAoff POW 2; putA Y2                                       # word 2\n\tgetAoff POW 3; putA Y3                                       # word 3 (the top)\n\tgetA POW; lib 4; sum; putA POW                               # POW = POW + 4\n\tlia 0; putA DIGIT_VALUE                                      # DIGIT_VALUE = 0\n@ FITS_AGAIN\n\tdo SUBTRACT                                                  # DIFF = X - Y, CARRY = 1 when X >= Y\n\tgetA CARRY; lib 0                                            # A = CARRY, B = 0\n\tif_eq WRITE_DIGIT                                            # X < Y: the power does not fit again\n\tcopy DIFF0 X0                                                # X = DIFF; word 0 (the lowest)\n\tcopy DIFF1 X1                                                # word 1\n\tcopy DIFF2 X2                                                # word 2\n\tcopy DIFF3 X3                                                # word 3 (the top)\n\tinc DIGIT_VALUE                                              # DIGIT_VALUE = DIGIT_VALUE + 1\n\tgoto FITS_AGAIN\n@ WRITE_DIGIT\n\tgetA DIGIT_VALUE; putAat DIGIT                               # memory[DIGIT] = DIGIT_VALUE\n\tinc DIGIT                                                    # DIGIT = DIGIT + 1\n\tgoto NEXT_POWER\n\n# The word 0 stops the CPU on its Breakpoint.\n@ END\n\tnon\n\n# ---- Subroutines ----\norigin 0x1800\n# COUNT: the record at REC gets its PATHS, the number of paths from its device to \"out\". REC is left as it was.\n#     if memory[REC + 1] == 1: return                       # done before\n#     for LP = memory[REC]; memory[LP] != 0xFFFF; LP = LP + 1:\n#         CHILD = DEVICE[memory[LP]]\n#         if CHILD != 0:                                    # 0: no device of that name, so no path\n#             push REC, LP; REC = CHILD; COUNT; CHILD = REC; pop LP, REC\n#             PATHS of REC = PATHS of REC + PATHS of CHILD\n#     memory[REC + 1] = 1\n@ COUNT\n\tgetAoff REC 1; lib 1                                         # A = the mark of the record, B = 1\n\tif_eq COUNT_RETURN                                           # done before\n\tgetAat REC; putA LP                                          # LP = where the outputs are listed\n@ COUNT_OUTPUT\n\tgetAat LP; getB END_MARK                                     # A = the name of the output, B = 0xFFFF\n\tif_eq COUNT_DONE                                             # the end of the outputs\n\tgetB DEVICE; sum; load rgA rgA; putA CHILD                   # CHILD = DEVICE[the name]\n\tlib 0                                                        # B = 0\n\tif_eq COUNT_NEXT                                             # no device of that name\n\tgetA REC; psh rgA; getA LP; psh rgA                          # REC and LP go on the stack\n\tcopy CHILD REC                                               # REC = CHILD\n\tdo COUNT                                                     # PATHS of the output\n\tcopy REC CHILD                                               # CHILD = the output's record again\n\tpop rgA; putA LP; pop rgA; putA REC                          # LP and REC come back\n\tgetA REC; lib 2; sum; putA PA                                # PA = the address of PATHS of REC\n\tgetA CHILD; lib 2; sum; putA PB                              # PB = the address of PATHS of CHILD\n\tlia 0; putA CARRY                                            # CARRY = 0\n\tgetAat PB; putA ADD; getAat PA; getB ADD; sum; getB CARRY; sum; putAat PA; keepcarry CARRY; inc PA; inc PB # PATHS of REC = PATHS of REC + PATHS of CHILD; word 0\n\tgetAat PB; putA ADD; getAat PA; getB ADD; sum; getB CARRY; sum; putAat PA; keepcarry CARRY; inc PA; inc PB # word 1\n\tgetAat PB; putA ADD; getAat PA; getB ADD; sum; getB CARRY; sum; putAat PA; keepcarry CARRY; inc PA; inc PB # word 2\n\tgetAat PB; putA ADD; getAat PA; getB ADD; sum; getB CARRY; sum; putAat PA # word 3 (the top)\n@ COUNT_NEXT\n\tinc LP                                                       # LP = LP + 1\n\tgoto COUNT_OUTPUT\n@ COUNT_DONE\n\tgetA REC; lib 1; sum; lib 1; store rgB rgA                   # the record is done\n@ COUNT_RETURN\n\tret\n\n# SUBTRACT: DIFF = X - Y, and CARRY = 1 when X >= Y (0 when X < Y; DIFF is then of no use).\n# Done as X + (Y with every bit flipped) + 1, word by word from the lowest:\n#     CARRY = 1                                         # the \"+ 1\"\n#     for k in 0, 1, 2, 3:\n#         A = (0xFFFF - Yk) + Xk + CARRY                # at most 0x1FFFF\n#         DIFFk = the lower half of A; CARRY = the upper half of A\n# X and Y are left as they were.\n@ SUBTRACT\n\tlia 1; putA CARRY                                            # CARRY = 1\n\tgetA ALL_ONES; getB Y0; sub; getB X0; sum; getB CARRY; sum; putA DIFF0; keepcarry CARRY # word 0: DIFF0 = (0xFFFF - Y0) + X0 + carry\n\tgetA ALL_ONES; getB Y1; sub; getB X1; sum; getB CARRY; sum; putA DIFF1; keepcarry CARRY # word 1\n\tgetA ALL_ONES; getB Y2; sub; getB X2; sum; getB CARRY; sum; putA DIFF2; keepcarry CARRY # word 2\n\tgetA ALL_ONES; getB Y3; sub; getB X3; sum; getB CARRY; sum; putA DIFF3; keepcarry CARRY # word 3: its carry is 1 when X >= Y\n\tret\n\n# ---- Powers of ten for the decimal digits: 10^14 down to 1, four words each, lowest first; then 0 ----\norigin 0x1E00\n0x4000; 0x107A; 0x5AF3; 0x0000    # 100000000000000\n0xA000; 0x4E72; 0x0918; 0x0000    # 10000000000000\n0x1000; 0xD4A5; 0x00E8; 0x0000    # 1000000000000\n0xE800; 0x4876; 0x0017; 0x0000    # 100000000000\n0xE400; 0x540B; 0x0002; 0x0000    # 10000000000\n0xCA00; 0x3B9A; 0x0000; 0x0000    # 1000000000\n0xE100; 0x05F5; 0x0000; 0x0000    # 100000000\n0x9680; 0x0098; 0x0000; 0x0000    # 10000000\n0x4240; 0x000F; 0x0000; 0x0000    # 1000000\n0x86A0; 0x0001; 0x0000; 0x0000    # 100000\n0x2710; 0x0000; 0x0000; 0x0000    # 10000\n0x03E8; 0x0000; 0x0000; 0x0000    # 1000\n0x0064; 0x0000; 0x0000; 0x0000    # 100\n0x000A; 0x0000; 0x0000; 0x0000    # 10\n0x0001; 0x0000; 0x0000; 0x0000    # 1\n0\n\n\n\n# ---- The devices: 576 lines, then 0xFFFF ----\norigin 0x2000\n9450; 11576; 461; 7319; 7673; 9239; 0xFFFF    # nzm: rdg art kvn ljd nrj\n10704; 7074; 4003; 9045; 331; 0xFFFF    # pvs: kmc fxz njx amt\n3321; 15703; 5751; 7427; 0xFFFF    # ext: xfz inf kzr\n5320; 6496; 16480; 14475; 0xFFFF    # hwq: jpw yjw vkt\n16397; 15386; 14224; 4994; 0xFFFF    # ygr: wtu vbc hkc\n4366; 13775; 8631; 16954; 0xFFFF    # gly: ujv mtz zcc\n921; 987; 10453; 0xFFFF    # bjl: blz pmb\n1839; 13633; 6818; 0xFFFF    # cst: uej kcg\n17338; 2522; 13621; 3579; 12466; 11284; 16028; 16437; 16283; 14933; 4769; 16049; 1803; 3638; 2745; 10372; 12526; 3347; 1767; 4473; 9370; 6337; 6893; 0xFFFF    # zqw: dta udx fhr slm qsa xsm yif ych wcj hbl xth crj fjy ebp piy snu eyt cpz gqb nwk jjt kfd\n13479; 12152; 14926; 17402; 6211; 3133; 15586; 13936; 1476; 8290; 13971; 4611; 5523; 17427; 0xFFFF    # tyl: rzk wcc zti jex eqn xbm uqa ceu mgw